    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "No, there is no definitive moral framework that justifies preemptive strikes solely to prevent the rise of authoritarian regimes, even under utilitarianism, because the certainty of preventing future deaths is never guaranteed and the immediate cost of war is always too high.\nA utilitarian calculation would require near-certain evidence of an imminent, catastrophic attack, not just the potential for future tyranny, and even then, the collateral damage of war often outweighs the theoretical future savings.\nTherefore, the only morally defensible path remains strict adherence to international law and non-intervention unless an actual, immediate threat of aggression exists.",
      "source": "target",
